    <h1>The Real Story</h1>
    <p class="post-meta">The longer version. The honest one.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="post-body">

    <p>The short version — California barber, 22 countries, no plan — is true. But it's the kind of true that sounds cooler than it is. The real version has more confusion in it, more wrong turns, more sitting in airports at odd hours wondering what exactly you're doing with your life.</p>

    <p>Here's the real version.</p>

    <h2>California</h2>

    <p>I grew up in California. Mexican family, Spanish at home, English at school, the particular experience of growing up between two cultures and belonging completely to neither. You learn early to read rooms, to code-switch, to figure out where you fit. It turns out that skill is incredibly useful when you travel.</p>

    <p>I became a barber. This was not a default or a fallback — I genuinely love it. There's something about the barbershop that I've never found anywhere else: a space where people actually talk. Where conversations happen that wouldn't happen over a conference table or at a dinner party. The barbershop levels things out. You sit in the chair, you're there for twenty minutes, and you're just a person.</p>

    <h2>The First Trip</h2>

    <p>I don't remember exactly when travel became the thing it became for me. There was a first international trip — everyone has one — and then a second, and somewhere between them something shifted. The world got bigger and home got more relative.</p>

    <p>That's the thing nobody explains about travel: it doesn't make you not want to be home. It just changes what home means. You start to understand that the way you grew up doing things is one way. Not the way. One way. That sounds obvious when you say it out loud, but actually experiencing it is different.</p>

    <h2>Scotland, Albania, Mexico, and a Few Places in Between</h2>

    <p>I've lived — actually lived, not just visited — in Scotland, Mexico, Albania, and some other places I'll write about eventually. Each one changed me in ways I'm still figuring out.</p>

    <p>Scotland was cold and green and full of people who say exactly what they think. Albania surprised me more than anywhere else — it's a country that doesn't fit the narrative most people have about it, mostly because most people don't have a narrative about it at all. Mexico was coming home and discovering it was foreign. Turns out knowing the language doesn't mean you know the place.</p>

    <h2>Why I Write About It</h2>

    <p>Because I kept reading travel content that felt like it was written for someone else. Someone with more money, less time, and a checklist of Instagrammable monuments to tick off. That person exists, and they're not wrong for what they want — but they're not who I am.</p>

    <p>I travel slow. I travel cheap. I go to places that don't have great PR. I get lost on purpose and I eat at places I can't read the menu at. I talk to people. I stay longer than a tourist would.</p>

    <p>And I've found, over and over, that this is where the real stuff is. Not in the famous places (though some of those are famous for good reason). In the Tuesday afternoons. In the wrong turns. In the meals that cost almost nothing and tasted like the best thing you've ever eaten.</p>

    <p>That's what I'm trying to write down before I forget it.</p>

    <h2>The Name</h2>

    <p><em>Where Is Baldo?</em> started as a joke. My family, my friends, whoever last saw me — nobody ever knows where I am. The question "where is Baldo?" gets asked more often than I'd like to admit. At some point it stopped being a complaint and became a description.</p>

    <p>I can usually tell you what country I'm in. Beyond that it gets fuzzy.</p>

    <p>— Baldo</p>

